<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SPFN Mobile — the Compose example app's window theme.

  One attribute over a system parent, and both halves of that are the decision.

  The PARENT is the theme this app already named in its manifest, unchanged:
  Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.NoActionBar. DeviceDefault and not Material or AppCompat,
  because this repository depends on no design system it did not write (decision C2) and
  either of those would be a new artifact in gradle/verification-metadata.xml for a window
  background. Light and not the appearance-following variant, because `SpfnTokens` resolves
  to the same white palette in both appearances: a theme that went dark on a dark-mode
  device would paint `FlowHost.cover()` — which reads `android:colorBackground` off this
  theme, and is the one attribute every Android theme defines — near-black behind screens
  that are still white. Declaring the parent here rather than in the manifest changes
  nothing about what that attribute resolves to.

  The ATTRIBUTE is `android:windowLightStatusBar`, and it says what the palette above has
  already decided: this app's background is white in both appearances, so the status bar's
  clock and icons have to be drawn dark. Left unset they follow the system's appearance, and
  on a dark-mode Galaxy Z Flip4 that meant a light foreground on this app's white — the
  clock and the battery legible only at an angle on the menu and the sheets, and invisible
  over a pushed screen.

  It is declared and not set in code. `WindowInsetsControllerCompat` would do the same job
  from the activity, at the cost of a rule that only holds once a composition has run and of
  a second place the appearance is decided. The window is the app's, and this is the app
  saying what its window is.

  `android:windowLightNavigationBar` is deliberately ABSENT. It is API 27 and this
  repository's minSdk is 24, so it would need a values-v27 split or a lint suppression to
  say something that is already true: a three-button navigation bar is drawn on the system's
  own grey with dark icons, and the gesture pill takes its colour from the content behind
  it. There is nothing here for it to fix.
-->
<resources>

    <style name="Theme.SpfnExample" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
